From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 12:45:30 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vkr: bound the idle ring wait so a lost guest wake-up cannot hang it

An idle venus ring thread sleeps in cnd_wait() until the guest sends
vkNotifyRingMESA. The guest sends that only when it reads IDLE in the
ring status after publishing its tail, and it does not re-send while it
then waits for a reply: vn_ring_wait_seqno() -> vn_relax() polls the
head and nothing else. If one wake-up is lost between the guest and this
thread, each side waits for the other until the guest aborts by design
after ~895 s (vn_relax, abort_order).

That is the state captured four times on Raspberry Pi 4 (Xen, DomA AAOS
WebView, DomD render server) on 2026-10-01: every vkr-ring-N thread in
futex, pending_notify clear, qemu idle, and the guest blocked in
vkGetMemoryResourcePropertiesMESA -- a handler that cannot block, so the
command had never been dispatched at all. The browser either froze for
15 minutes and then died in vn_relax, or was killed earlier by an input
ANR.

Bound the wait at 10 ms. A bounded wake that finds the ring still empty
goes straight back to sleep with IDLE left set, so an idle ring costs
one timed wake per period rather than the 1 ms re-poll a real wake-up
performs. A bounded wake that finds queued commands with no notify is
counted and logged ("no wake-up from the guest"): that count measures
how often the wake-up is lost on this transport, which is the part still
not explained.

The ring condition variable is initialised with CLOCK_MONOTONIC so a
wall-clock step (DomD sets its clock from NTP after boot) can neither
shorten the bound nor stretch it into a hang.

Note for anyone touching this: this tree's c11 shim returns thrd_busy on
ETIMEDOUT, not thrd_timeout. A comparison with thrd_timeout alone is
constant false and the compiler deletes the recovery branch -- the first
build of this patch did exactly that, and only a check for the log
string in the binary caught it.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/venus/vkr_ring.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_ring.c
@@ -8,11 +8,67 @@
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <sys/resource.h>
 #include <time.h>
+#include <pthread.h>
 
 #include "venus-protocol/vn_protocol_renderer_dispatches.h"
 
 #include "vkr_context.h"
 
+/*
+ * BOUNDED IDLE WAIT.
+ *
+ * An idle ring thread used to sleep in cnd_wait() until the guest sent
+ * vkNotifyRingMESA. The guest only sends that when it reads IDLE in the ring status
+ * after publishing its tail, and it does not re-send while it then waits for a
+ * reply (vn_ring_wait_seqno -> vn_relax polls the head and nothing else). So if one
+ * wake-up is lost between the guest and this thread, both sides wait for each other:
+ * the guest's call never starts here, and the guest aborts by design after ~895 s
+ * (vn_relax, abort_order).
+ *
+ * That is exactly the state captured four times on RPi4 DomA/DomD (2026-10-01):
+ * every vkr-ring-N thread in futex, pending_notify clear, qemu idle, and the guest
+ * blocked in vkGetMemoryResourcePropertiesMESA -- a handler that cannot block, so the
+ * command had simply never been dispatched.
+ *
+ * Bounding the wait turns a lost wake-up into a delay of at most
+ * VKR_RING_IDLE_WAIT_MAX_NS. A bounded wake that finds the ring still empty goes
+ * straight back to sleep with IDLE left set, so an idle ring costs one timed wake
+ * per period and not the 1 ms re-poll a real wake-up performs. A bounded wake that
+ * finds queued commands and no notify is counted and logged: that count is the
+ * measurement of how often the wake-up is lost on this transport.
+ */
+#define VKR_RING_IDLE_WAIT_MAX_NS (10ull * 1000 * 1000)
+
+/* The ring condition variable is waited on with a CLOCK_MONOTONIC deadline, so a
+ * wall-clock step (DomD sets its clock from NTP after boot) can neither cut the
+ * bound short nor stretch it into a hang. cnd_t is pthread_cond_t in this tree. */
+static int
+vkr_ring_cnd_init_monotonic(cnd_t *cond)
+{
+   pthread_condattr_t attr;
+   if (pthread_condattr_init(&attr))
+      return thrd_error;
+   int ret = pthread_condattr_setclock(&attr, CLOCK_MONOTONIC);
+   if (!ret)
+      ret = pthread_cond_init(cond, &attr);
+   pthread_condattr_destroy(&attr);
+   return ret ? thrd_error : thrd_success;
+}
+
+static void
+vkr_ring_note_lost_notify(struct vkr_ring *ring, uint32_t queued)
+{
+   static atomic_uint_fast64_t lost;
+   const uint64_t n = atomic_fetch_add(&lost, 1) + 1;
+   /* the first few always, then sparsely: enough to count without flooding */
+   if (n <= 16 || (n & (n - 1)) == 0)
+      vkr_log("ring %u: no wake-up from the guest, %u byte(s) queued; recovered on the "
+              "%llu ms bound (lost wake-ups so far: %llu)",
+              (unsigned)ring->id, queued,
+              (unsigned long long)(VKR_RING_IDLE_WAIT_MAX_NS / 1000000),
+              (unsigned long long)n);
+}
+
 static inline void *
 get_resource_pointer(const struct vkr_resource *res, size_t offset)
 {
@@ -142,7 +198,7 @@
    if (mtx_init(&ring->mutex, mtx_plain) != thrd_success)
       goto err_mtx_init;
 
-   if (cnd_init(&ring->cond) != thrd_success)
+   if (vkr_ring_cnd_init_monotonic(&ring->cond) != thrd_success)
       goto err_cond_init;
 
    return ring;
@@ -274,8 +330,30 @@
          TRACE_SCOPE("ring idle");
 
          mtx_lock(&ring->mutex);
-         if (ring->started && !ring->pending_notify)
-            cnd_wait(&ring->cond, &ring->mutex);
+         bool timed_out = false;
+         if (ring->started && !ring->pending_notify) {
+            struct timespec deadline;
+            clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &deadline);
+            const uint64_t ns = (uint64_t)deadline.tv_nsec + VKR_RING_IDLE_WAIT_MAX_NS;
+            deadline.tv_sec += ns / 1000000000ull;
+            deadline.tv_nsec = ns % 1000000000ull;
+            /* This tree's c11 shim returns thrd_busy, not thrd_timeout, on
+             * ETIMEDOUT (src/mesa/compat/c11/threads_posix.h). Comparing with
+             * thrd_timeout alone is always false -- the compiler then deletes the
+             * whole recovery branch, which is what the first build of this patch
+             * did. Accept both so a fixed shim cannot silently break it again. */
+            const int wr = cnd_timedwait(&ring->cond, &ring->mutex, &deadline);
+            timed_out = wr == thrd_busy || wr == thrd_timeout;
+         }
+         if (timed_out && ring->started && !ring->pending_notify) {
+            const uint32_t queued = vkr_ring_load_tail(ring) - ring->buffer.cur;
+            if (!queued) {
+               /* still empty: stay idle, IDLE stays set, no 1 ms re-poll */
+               mtx_unlock(&ring->mutex);
+               continue;
+            }
+            vkr_ring_note_lost_notify(ring, queued);
+         }
          vkr_ring_unset_status_bits(ring, VK_RING_STATUS_IDLE_BIT_MESA);
          mtx_unlock(&ring->mutex);
 
